{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["(verses 1–11) <b>And you shall answer and say.</b> He said <b>and you shall answer</b> — there are those who interpret it as an expression of beginning, like (Job 3:2) ״וַיַּעַן אִיּוֹב״. And it is possible that it means answering in the sense of a lowly person who has received great goodness from a king, humbling himself before Him with a broken heart; so too he humbles himself before HaShem and says: ״אֲרַמִּי אֹבֵד אָבִי״ — for our father was lost and wandering and exiled in Egypt, with meager numbers etc., and HaShem took us out of Egypt and brought us etc. And corresponding to this statement, which contains elevation and dignity, he said <b>and you shall say</b>. And similar to this our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Pesachim 116b) regarding the Passover Haggadah: one begins with disgrace and concludes with praise." "<b>And I have peered</b> into this parashah, that it alludes as well to entry into the supernal land. And He said <b>and it shall be when you come</b> — for it is not fitting for a person to rejoice except when he comes to the supernal land, along the lines of (Proverbs 31:25) ״וְתִשְׂחַק לְיוֹם אַחֲרוֹן״, for the joy of this world is nothing but vanity and pursuit of wind. And He turned back and specified about which land He is speaking, and said <b>which HaShem your Elohim gives you as a heritage</b> — for to this is called an eternal heritage. And He was precise in saying <b>gives you</b>, for in comparison to what the supernal world is worth, all that a person acquires on his own behalf does not reach even a part in a thousand thousands of its worth. Therefore He said ״gives you״ in the manner of a gift. And because a person must labor in this world to fulfill Torah and commandments, for this He said ״וִירִשְׁתָּהּ״ [and you shall possess it]." "And His saying <b>and you shall dwell in it</b> — along the lines of what they said (Berakhot 35a): the righteous sit and their crowns are on their heads." "And His saying <b>and you shall take</b> etc. — along the lines of what they said in the Zohar (vol. 2, 59a), that all that a person does of the commandments and good deeds while he is alive gathers together hand upon hand until the day of his departure, and with them he is received before the King, in the manner of what is said (Isaiah 58:8) ״וְהָלַךְ לְפָנֶיךָ צִדְקֶךָ״. And this is what He said <b>and you shall take of the first of every fruit</b> etc. — meaning, he shall not take in his hand to greet HaShem except from the choicest things he did in this world. It further alludes to the Torah, which is called רֵאשִׁית [first] (Bereshit Rabbah 1:1)." "And His saying <b>which HaShem your Elohim gives you</b> may be explained along the lines of what they said of blessed memory (Berakhot 35a): it is written ״לַה׳ הָאָרֶץ וּמְלוֹאָהּ״ (Psalms 24:1) and it is written ״וְהָאָרֶץ נָתַן לִבְנֵי אָדָם״ (Psalms 115:16) — there is no contradiction: here it is before a blessing, here it is after a blessing — end quote. Behold that after a person does what HaShem commanded him, the matter is reckoned as though through his agency the deed was done that it is given to him as a gift; for without this, all of a person's deeds have no advantage for us, for since all his labor is through the agency of the world in which he is, and to HaShem belongs the earth and its fullness — therefore He said ״which HaShem your Elohim gives you.״" "And the verse's saying <b>and you shall place in the basket</b>. It alludes that all the good deeds he has done must be in accordance with their words, as it says (Deuteronomy 17:11) ״עַל פִּי הַתּוֹרָה אֲשֶׁר יוֹרוּךָ וְעַל הַמִּשְׁפָּט״ and so forth — and the Torah and the laws, the sages explained them in sixty tractates, corresponding to the numerical value of טנא (basket); apart from this, even if he performs all the commandments in the world, he and they [the deeds] will be burned." "And the verse's saying <b>and you shall go to the place</b> and so forth — for upon the soul's ascent to the supernal world, it does not proceed immediately and at once to encounter the Presence of HaShem, until it prepares itself in the corridor. And some are delayed seven days, or a month, or [several] days, as stated in the Zohar (vol. 1, 81a), and afterward it ascends to the supernal Garden of Eden, which is called צרור החיים — and that is the place which HaShem shall choose to cause His name to dwell there." "And the verse's saying <b>and you shall come to the kohen</b>. In accordance with what they said, of blessed memory (Chagigah 12b), that Michael stands to serve as kohen before HaShem, to offer the souls before the Elohim of eternity — and to him he shall say these words: ״I have declared this day״ and so forth." "And the verse's saying <b>who shall be in those days</b> may be explained according to their words, which they said, that the kohen standing there changes. Go out and learn what they said in Zohar Chadash (parashat Lech Lecha, folio 34) concerning Michael — when did he merit to be in this station? It was from the time he brought the soul of David up to the supernal Garden of Eden, after Solomon built the Holy Temple, and so forth. You see, then, that at times the kohen changes; therefore it says ״who shall be in those days.״ <b>And you shall say to him: I declared this day to HaShem that I have come to the land</b> — this is the land from which he came. And it says <b>HaShem swore to give us</b> — this is the land of Israel, through whose instrumentality they achieve fulfillment of Torah and commandments and good deeds." "And the verse's saying <b>and the kohen shall take the basket from your hand</b> — meaning, that the supernal minister accepts only what comes in his hand, founded upon the words of the Oral Torah, which is alluded to in the word טנא (basket), as we have written, ״and he shall set it down before״ and so forth." "<b>And you shall answer and say, etc.</b> Meaning, for all that a person does of good deeds in this world is not sufficient to satisfy the supreme King. Go and learn from what our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Eruvin 17a): if He were to enter into judgment with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, they would not be able to stand before Him, for what can mortal man justify? And it is written (Job 4:17): ״אִם מֵעוֹשֵׂהוּ יִטְהַר גָּבֶר״ — therefore the Torah said that one should give an answer to HaShem and a defense for not having fulfilled His will, blessed be He, as required, and this is the verse's saying <b>and you shall answer and say before HaShem: Aramean lost my father.</b> Aramean — this is the evil inclination, for it is a great deceiver, as it says (Genesis 3:1): ״וְהַנָּחָשׁ הָיָה עָרוּם״, who deceives creatures to destroy them. And this is what it means by <b>lost my father</b> — meaning the first man, along the lines of (Isaiah 43:27): ״אָבִיךָ הָרִאשׁוֹן חָטָא״ — and he destroyed him and brought death upon him and upon the generations that came after him, and implanted in all souls the portion of evil, and this is the sod of the orlah (foreskin) that cleaves to a person from the womb and from conception. It further intends by <b>lost my father</b> along the lines of (Psalms 14:1): ״אָמַר נָבָל בְּלִבּוֹ אֵין אֱלֹהִים״." "<b>Furthermore,</b> it may intend by <b>my father</b> — that is the soul, for all the counsels of the Satan are to destroy the soul. And the soul is called av (father), as they say in the Zohar, parashat Lekh Lekha (77b), and this is what prevents a person from the obligatory deeds in the service of HaShem. And not only this, but <b>וַיֵּרֶד מִצְרַיְמָה</b> — meaning the place into which the evil inclination comes, which is a person's body, which is the narrow straits (מְצֵרִים) of the evil inclination that are close to it, in the sod of (Isaiah 65:25): ״וְנָחָשׁ עָפָר לַחְמוֹ״, and it desires whatever it desires of the matters of this world and its tastes which HaShem has made repugnant, for the four elements within it have, after the sin of man, become materialized and defiled, and for this reason HaShem decreed death upon man. And through this its words and enticements are heard among the elements of a person. Also the body is called Mitzrayim (Egypt) because it constrains (מֵצֵר) the soul with its desires." "And the verse's saying <b>and he sojourned there</b> — for at first it enters as a sojourner, as it says (II Samuel 12:4): ״וַיָּבֹא הֵלֶךְ״ etc. (Sukkah 52b)." "<b>In few people</b> — meaning with few powers, and afterward its powers multiply through the person himself, and this is the verse's saying <b>and he became there a great and mighty nation</b>, <b>and numerous</b> — in the sod of (Psalms 25:11): ״וְסָלַחְתָּ לַעֲוֹנִי כִּי רַב הוּא״, and the Ari, of blessed memory, explained that it returns to the seducer, for it is great (כִּי רַב הוּא), and the proof is that HaShem commanded on Yom HaKippurim one lot for HaShem and one lot for Azazel (Leviticus 16:8)." "And the verse's saying <b>״וַיָּרֵעוּ אֹתָנוּ הַמִּצְרִים״ etc.</b> — meaning, that through the intermediary of a person's elements they also caused the soul to act wickedly like them, and this is the verse's saying <b>וַיָּרֵעוּ אֹתָנוּ</b> — meaning, they made us evil like them, for when the temperament spoils it draws toward itself the will of the soul." "And the verse's saying <b>וַיְעַנּוּנוּ</b> — this alludes to the exhaustion of strength of the one who battles against the evil inclination. Go and learn from the incident of Rav Amram the Pious (Kiddushin 81a), who did not have the strength to stand firm against the one who causes sin until he cried out, ״A fire in the house of Amram!״ and so forth." "And the verse's saying <b>וַיִּתְּנוּ עָלֵינוּ עֲבוֹדָה קָשָׁה</b> — meaning, that the evil inclination does not conduct itself in this manner only at widely spaced intervals, but constantly, at every hour and every moment, it overpowers a person; and therefore it is called עֲבוֹדָה קָשָׁה (hard labor). Further, it intends in accordance with their words of blessed memory (Kiddushin 30b), who said that every day the evil inclination is renewed upon a person with renewed force, and corresponding to this it says <b>עֲבוֹדָה קָשָׁה.</b> All these things a servant of Israel should say before the Creator to find deliverance for a small offering." "And the verse's saying <b>״וַנִּצְעַק אֶל ה׳״</b> — here it alludes that a person needs to pray to HaShem every day that He deliver him from the evil inclination, and that is its saying <b>״וַנִּצְעַק אֶל ה׳״</b> and so forth. And its saying <b>״וַיִּשְׁמַע ה׳ אֶת קוֹלֵנוּ״</b> and so forth — meaning, even though it is HaShem who created the evil inclination to test a person in the service of his Creator, nevertheless when one cries out to Him He hears their voice, because He sees the impression — which is our affliction, our toil, and our oppression. The affliction is the exhaustion of the strength of the combatant, that there is not sufficient strength in us to battle it and banish it from ourselves; the toil is that we must labor in warring against it; the oppression is the coercion with which the evil inclination pressures a person to sin against his will — and for these three reasons HaShem heard our voice. Further, it intends: ״our affliction״ — this is the poverty that accompanies Torah scholars, as they said of blessed memory (Midrash Tehillim 5) that the Torah said: ״Why are My children poor?״ And even if a Torah scholar is found upon whom the rod of poverty does not rest, nevertheless he should conduct himself with the trait of poverty, as they said (Avot ch. 6): ״Such is the way of Torah״ and so forth. ״And our toil״ — in the manner of their saying (Berakhot 17a): ״Happy is he whose toil is in Torah״ and so forth, and all the more so in the later generations, whose toil is great to bring to light one ruling or one halakhah. ״And our oppression״ — for all who are attached to the Torah, those who oppress them multiply, and the eyes of all look to him." "And the verse's saying <b>וַיּוֹצִיאֵנוּ ה׳ מִמִּצְרַיִם</b> — this is the deliverance from the evil inclination and its forces that oppress (מְצֵרִים) us, for He is the one who delivers from the evil inclination, as they said of blessed memory (Kiddushin 30b): were it not that HaShem helps a person against the evil inclination, no person is able to save himself from it, as it is written: ״צוֹפֶה רָשָׁע״ — this is the evil inclination — ״לַצַּדִּיק לַהֲמִיתוֹ, ה׳ לֹא יַעַזְבֶנּוּ בְיָדוֹ״ (Psalms 37:32)." "And the verse's saying <b>בְּיָד חֲזָקָה</b> and so forth — it alludes to the things through whose intermediary HaShem delivers him and so forth, and this is in the manner of their words of blessed memory (Kiddushin 30b): the Holy One, blessed be He, said: ״I created for you the evil inclination; I created for it the antidote, which is the Torah.״ And that is the saying of <b>וַיּוֹצִיאֵנוּ ה׳</b> and so forth — <b>בְּיָד חֲזָקָה</b>: this is the Torah, which was given from the right hand of the Holy One, blessed be He, as it is written (Deuteronomy 33:2): ״מִימִינוֹ אֵשׁ דָּת לָמוֹ״, and it was also given in mighty acts from amidst the fire." "And the verse's saying <b>and with an outstretched arm</b> etc. alludes to three things through which a person is saved from the evil inclination, as they said, of blessed memory (Menachot 43b): whoever has tefillin on his arm, tzitzit on his garment, and a mezuzah at his doorway will not quickly sin. Corresponding to tefillin on his arm, it says <b>and with an outstretched arm</b>, for the commandment of tefillin is on the arm, and it says <b>and with great terror</b> corresponding to the tefillin of the head. And corresponding to tzitzit it says <b>and with signs</b>, for the commandment of tzitzit is a sign for us that we are servants of the Holy One, blessed be He, as we explained in the section of tzitzit (Numbers 15:37); also it may be called thus in allusion to the tekhelet, in which, as the Ramban wrote (Numbers 15:38), it alludes to the comprehensive attribute that is called 'signs.' And corresponding to the mezuzah it says <b>and with wonders</b> according to its plain meaning; also the mezuzah may be called a wonder (mofet), for it is a wonder to remove death from Israel, as they said in the Zohar (Zohar III 265a), that many bands of the kelipot are terrified when they see the name Shaddai on the doorway of an Israelite." "<b>Further</b>, it is possible that the entire verse alludes to the Torah, through which a person is saved from the evil inclination and from the desires of this world, and it states five matters corresponding to the five books of the Torah: <b>with a strong hand</b> corresponds to the book of Genesis, for there the work of the hands of the Creator is stated, whose hands established the world and its fullness; and it says <b>and with an outstretched arm</b> corresponding to the book of Exodus, for in it He bared His arm and showed His mighty deeds in the deeds of Egypt and at the Sea and in the giving of the Torah; and it says <b>and with great terror</b> corresponding to Leviticus, for there He commanded the work of [the service of] the Tabernacle and the dwelling of His Shekhinah, and He commanded regarding awe of the Place and sanctified the House through two great ones of Israel, and therefore this book is associated with great terror; and it says <b>and with signs</b> corresponding to the book of Numbers, on account of (Numbers 2:2) ״אִישׁ עַל דִּגְלוֹ בְאֹתֹת״; and it says <b>and with wonders</b> corresponding to the book of Deuteronomy, for in it Moses spoke to all Israel and admonished them with wonders (mofetim) about their recklessness and about the goodness and lovingkindness of HaShem toward them, and he admonished that HaShem did not repay them according to their evil deeds and His lovingkindness prevailed over them; also in it are stated the wonders of the prophets through which they vindicate the prophet." "And the verse's saying <b>and He brought us to this place</b> speaks of the supernal world where the soul dwells. Now, the soul does not attain to speak all these words except after its ascent to the supernal world, which is called the Garden of Eden on high, after it has already been sated for seven days in the Garden of Eden below. Therefore, corresponding to its standing before HaShem to speak these words, it says <b>and He brought us to this place</b>, for the soul's standing there alone holds great delight. And corresponding to the Garden of Eden below, which it has already merited, it says <b>and He gave us this land, a land flowing with milk and honey</b>, in the manner of the verse (Song of Songs 4:11) ״דְּבַשׁ וְחָלָב תַּחַת לְשׁוֹנֵךְ״." "And the verse's saying <b>and now, behold</b> — the meaning of its saying 'and now' is an expression of repentance, that one returns before HaShem concerning the fine details of the commandments. And even though the verse speaks of one who has already merited the Garden of Eden below, nevertheless, in order to merit an supernal boundary, they will be examined with greater scrutiny. And this is the sod of the judgment that HaShem will render in the future in the Valley of Jehoshaphat (Midrash Tehillim 8), from which Samuel trembled and was afraid when the mistress of the ov raised him (Chagigah 4b)." "And the verse's saying <b>behold, I have brought the first of... which You have given me</b> — as I explained above regarding the phrase ״אֲשֶׁר ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ נוֹתֵן לָךְ״." "And the verse's saying <b>and you shall bow down before HaShem</b> — thereby he awakened a spirit of life into the wondrous good contained in the surpassing testimony that the righteous merit. Namely, that the soul of the righteous draws near before the delightful light and bows down before it with no veil separating. Happy is the soul of a person that reaches so surpassing a level! And this is what is said: <b>and you shall bow down before HaShem your Elohim</b> — there the soul recognizes its Elohim." "And He said immediately after this <b>and you shall rejoice in all the good</b>, after the manner of what is said (Psalms 145:9): ״טוֹב ה׳״ — and to awaken you to this He said ״in all the good,״ meaning the matter that encompasses all good, which is none other than the Holy One, blessed be He, who is the source of good. <b>which He has given you</b> — meaning that this matter has no comparison except as a free gift." "<b>And He has given you</b> — to the exclusion of anything else: no angel and no seraph merits this. Further, the phrase <b>in all the good</b> alludes to Torah, as they said, of blessed memory (Berakhot 5a): ״and there is no טוֹב but Torah,״ for if human beings could feel the sweetness and pleasantness of the goodness of Torah, they would go mad and burn with passion after it, and the whole world full of silver and gold would be considered in their eyes as nothing — for Torah encompasses all the good things in the world." "And the verse's saying <b>and to your house</b> — for the good reaches not the soul alone, but also the house of the soul, which is the ruach and nefesh that are the dwelling-place of the neshamah. Each one will be attuned according to its capacity." "And the verse's saying <b>and the Levite</b> — this is explained according to the words of the sages, of blessed memory, as they said in the Zohar (vol. II, p. 94a), and this is their language: ״זָכָה יַתִּיר יָהֲבִין לֵיהּ נִשְׁמְתָא — if he merits, they give him a soul... זָכָה יַתִּיר יָהֲבִין לֵיהּ נִשְׁמְתָא מֵאוֹר אֲצִילוּת — if he merits, they give him a soul from the light of Atzilut״ etc., until there. And for this aspect Scripture calls him לֵוִי, because it accompanies (מִתְלַוָּה) man constantly anew." "And the verse's saying <b>and the ger</b> may be explained according to the words of the masters of blessed memory (Zohar vol. 3, 117a), who said that when a mitzvah from among those mitzvot that do not apply constantly reaches a person's hand, those souls that have already departed from the world and are lacking this mitzvah come and sojourn with the one performing the mitzvah in this manner — close to the reward and far from the loss, as is stated at length in the words of the mekubalim. Corresponding to this spiritual reality, Scripture said: <b>and the ger</b> who is in your midst."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {13 "I read הַמְּפַתֶּה ('the seducer') as referring to the evil inclination / Satan, consistent with the thread of comments.", 18 "The Or HaChaim plays on מִמִּצְרַיִם ('from Egypt') and מְצֵרִים ('those who oppress/constrain'), a standard rabbinic wordplay retained in translation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:20:51.056809657Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}